WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.029
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hey, Pat, what's up?

00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:03.470
PAT: Priyanka, I'm in
crisis mode right now.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:04.850
My boss has asked me
to migrate to cloud,

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.000
and I don't know what to do.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.042
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK,
slow down and don't worry.

00:00:08.042 --> 00:00:10.710
In the next few minutes, we
will lay out the five easy steps

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.827
to kickstart your Google
Cloud migration journey.

00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:14.160
PAT: Wow, that would be awesome.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:14.953
Thanks so much.

00:00:14.953 --> 00:00:17.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, Pat,
hang on the line, and you all,

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.858
stay tuned.

00:00:18.858 --> 00:00:21.228
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:26.830
Hi, everyone.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.630
I'm Priyanka, and welcome
to Cloud Migration 101,

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.570
where we will talk about
all things migration.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.370
In this first
episode, I'll show you

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.530
how to get started with
your migration journey

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.480
to Google Cloud.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.410
What is cloud migration anyway?

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.170
Well, it typically refers
to moving apps deployed

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.210
on premises into
the cloud, maybe

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.880
to leverage the benefits
of cloud like reduced cost,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.980
increased security,
flexibility, and speed.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.620
It could also mean
moving workloads

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.840
from one cloud to another.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.910
Any type of moving
can be a challenge.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.400
Putting things into right
boxes, what if something gets

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.520
lost or breaks, can you
put it all back together?

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.310
Well, cloud migration
is no different, even

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.230
for the most experienced teams.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:09.830
So you need to plan and execute
your migration very carefully.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.220
Where do we get started?

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.130
Well, the first stop in
the migration journey

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.290
to Google Cloud town is to
define your starting point.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.340
Now, starting point
could be on premises

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.730
where you manage the entire
infrastructure and the apps.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.100
It could be in private
hosting environment,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.770
where you manage the
virtualization and resources,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.410
and the rest is managed
by the service provider.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.430
Or it could be in another cloud.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.310
Now that we have defined the
starting and the end points,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.080
we are ready to jump in to
stop number two, the types

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.730
of workloads to migrate.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.350
This is important
because it helps

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.750
us assess the complexity
of the migration.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.610
There can be different
kinds of workloads.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.230
But for our purposes
today, it is

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.870
safe to categorize them
as legacy workloads

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.220
and cloud native workloads.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.400
Legacy workloads are developed
without any consideration

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.660
for cloud environment.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.720
So it can be difficult and
frankly expensive to modify

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.400
them to run on cloud.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.260
On the other hand,
cloud-native workloads

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.160
are natively scalable,
portable, available, and secure.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.400
So consider the type of workload
you're dealing with while

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.600
planning a migration.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.370
Now, we are at stop three.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.710
It's time to pick the type of
migration that makes sense.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.860
There's lift and shift,
where you move workloads

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.930
from a source to a
target environment

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.300
with minor or no modifications.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.340
This is ideal when a
workload can operate

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.370
as is in the target environment,
or when there's little

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.770
or no business need for change.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.110
It also requires the
least amount of time

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.240
and are easiest to accomplish.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.600
The disadvantages-- you have
a non-cloud-native workload

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.820
running in Google
Cloud now, which is not

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.160
ideal to take full
advantage of Cloud Platform

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.040
features like horizontal
scaling, pricing,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.790
and managed services.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.660
The second type of migration
is improve and move,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.540
in which you modernize the
workload while migrating

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.900
it to take advantage of
cloud native capabilities.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.450
This may take longer than the
lift and shift, because you're

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.600
refactoring, but it turns out
to be better in the long run,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.450
because you gain access to many
of the cloud-native benefits.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.390
The third type is
rip and replace

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.660
migration, where you
decommission an existing app

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.540
and completely
redesign and rewrite it

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.490
as a cloud-native app.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.460
You would do this if your
current app is too expensive

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.490
to migrate, or you don't
want to maintain it.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.860
This will obviously
take a lot longer,

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.980
but the upside is this
migration sets you up

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.470
to take full advantage of
those amazing cloud features,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.760
including the horizontal
scaling, managed services,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.440
and high availability.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.720
Once you decide which
migration type makes

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.760
sense for your
business workload,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.090
we move one to the fourth stop
on our migration journey--

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.000
assess your readiness for cloud.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.140
Your readiness depends
on four themes--

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.810
learning programs,
leadership support,

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.530
experience to scale cloud-native
services, and the capacity

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.930
to protect your
current environment.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.290
For each theme, you should be
in one of the three phases--

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.970
tactical, strategic,
or transformational.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.220
Since this is an exercise
that highly depends

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.890
on your organization's
maturity for adopting cloud

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.050
technologies,
click here to learn

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.630
how to evaluate your
organization on the cloud

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:15.560
maturity scale.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.870
Stop five on our
journey is to define

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.260
the core of the migration,
the migration path.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.790
It has four phases.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.850
You assess, you plan, you
deploy, and then you optimize.

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:26.390
Got it?

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:26.890
No.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.300
Let me try that again
a little bit slower.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.780
In the assess phase, you
perform a thorough assessment

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.270
and discovery of your
existing environment

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.610
to understand your
app inventory,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.080
identify any dependencies
and requirements,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.830
perform total cost of
ownership calculations,

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.070
and establish app
performance benchmarks.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.880
In the plan phase, you create
the basic cloud infrastructure

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.450
for your apps and plan
how you will move them.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.140
This planning includes creating
an organization, folders,

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.020
environments,
projects, resources,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.770
and networks, along with access
controls, sorting the apps,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.100
and developing a prioritized
migration strategy.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.780
In deploy phase, you
design, implement,

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.770
and execute deployment
processes to move workloads

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.030
to Google Cloud.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.620
And in the optimize
phase, you'll

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.860
begin to take full advantage
of cloud-native technology,

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.520
such as performance,
scalability, disaster recovery,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.970
cost, training, as well as
opening the doors to machine

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.890
learning and
artificial intelligence

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:30.000
integrations for your apps.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.120
Here's the summary
of five steps we

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.100
defined to kickstart
your migration

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.480
journey to Google Cloud.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.670
First, you define the
starting point and end

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.380
point, which is Google Cloud.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.390
Then you define your
workloads followed

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.160
by the migration types.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.620
Then using the cloud
adoption framework,

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.170
assess your readiness
for your cloud journey.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.900
And finally, get set
for the migration path,

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.780
assess, plan,
deploy, and optimize.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.550
Now that we have
a starting point,

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:59.120
Pat is off building their
migration plan to Google Cloud.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.570
You all, stay tuned for the next
episode, where we will help you

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.790
choose what to migrate first.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:07.790
If you've got thoughts on
different methods you've used,

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:11.060
leave a comment below so we
can continue our conversation.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.460
Until then, like,
subscribe, to keep

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.380
learning about cloud migration.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.290
Oh, and check out the more
